## 6. Adversarial geometric regression

The virtual determinant mass from Target B §26.4 cannot be bounded by
`G+E+O(B)` for arbitrary triangle-free rhombic disks, even when the contact
complex is connected and simply connected. This is a **regression to a
static geometric shortcut only**; the example is far from globally diameter
minimizing.

For (m\ge4), set \(\theta_m=\pi/3+m^{-3}\), and let \(u,v\) be unit
vectors at angle \(\theta_m\). In lattice coordinates, take the cells

```text
0 <= j < m:  0 <= i < m  or  m+2 <= i < 2m+2,
j = m:      0 <= i < 2m+2.
```

This is two \(m\times m\) rhombus blocks separated by a two-column notch and
joined by a single top row. The union is a simply connected rhombic disk, with

```text
R=2m^2+2m+2,  B=8m+6,  T=B/2=4m+3,
n=R+T+1=2m^2+6m+6.
```

The source is the vertex set of this cell complex. Every nonzero lattice
vector \(pu+qv\) other than \(\pm u,\pm v\) has length strictly greater
than one: for opposite signs,
\(|pu+qv|^2=(|p|-|q|)^2+2|pq|(1-\cos\theta_m)>1\), with the
equal-magnitude case using \(2-2\cos\theta_m>1\). The unit-contact graph is
therefore bipartite in the lattice parity \(i+j\), so the source contains no
unit equilateral triangle.

The hull is the full parallelogram with lattice-coordinate side counts
`2m+2` and `m+1`. Hence

```text
G = 2m sin(theta_m),
E = R (sin(theta_m)-sqrt(3)/2)=O(1/m),
G+E=Theta(m),  c0 B=Theta(m),
(G+E)/(c0 B) -> 1/2.
```

Yet the `m` lower-left `u` trains and `m` lower-right `v` trains do not cross,
and the reverse pair of families also does not cross. Their virtual
determinant mass is

```text
M_inter >= 2m^2 sin(theta_m)=Theta(m^2).
```

Thus no fixed positive multiple of all inter-grain virtual determinant mass
can be paid by `G+E+O(B)` without a global-optimality or diameter-saturation
hypothesis. Indeed

```text
D_m^2=(5+4 cos(theta_m))(m+1)^2,
D_m^2/[c(n-1)] -> 7/(2c)>3,
```

so the translated triangular-lattice competitor has much smaller diameter for
the same cardinality. The construction gives no counterexample to the
global-minimizer shell theorem.

